{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "על האבנים", :translit "al ha-avanim", :gloss "on the stones"} {:he "וראיתן על האבנים", :translit "u-re'iten al ha-avanim", :gloss "and you shall see on the stones"} {:he "וארד בית היוצר והנה הוא עושה מלאכה על האבנים", :translit "va-ered beit ha-yotzer ve-hineh hu oseh melakhah al ha-avanim", :gloss "and I went down to the potter's house and behold he was doing work on the stones"} {:he "כי הנה כחומר ביד היוצר", :translit "ki hineh ka-homer be-yad ha-yotzer", :gloss "for behold like clay in the hand of the potter"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וראיתן על האבנים" "וארד בית היוצר והנה הוא עושה מלאכה על האבנים"]} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic analogy drawn from Jeremiah 18:3 to explain birth imagery"} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay linking יוצר (potter) to חומר (clay) via Jeremiah 18:6"}], :frame {:text "The phrase על האבנים occurs twice in Scripture: once in Exodus 1:16 and once in Jeremiah 18:3, where the prophet descends to the potter's house and sees him working על האבנים — on the stones (the potter's wheel). The Bavli in Sotah 11b cites Rabbi on the verse in Exodus: at the hour a woman crouches to give birth, her thighs cool like stones. The passage then draws a structural analogy to the potter (יוצר) from Jeremiah 18: just as the potter sits with a thigh on each side and the wheel (סדן) in the middle, so too a woman in labor has a thigh on each side and the child in the middle. A further interpretation connects the potter image to Jeremiah 18:6, כי הנה כחומר ביד היוצר — 'for behold, like clay in the hand of the potter.'", :sources ["Sotah 11b" "Jeremiah 18:3" "Jeremiah 18:6"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["וראיתן על האבנים" "וארד בית היוצר והנה הוא עושה מלאכה על האבנים"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "וראיתן על האבנים", :refs ["Exodus 1:16"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "וארד בית היוצר והנה הוא עושה מלאכה על האבנים", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insen